    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1259",
      "html": "<ul><li>Scientific name: Malapterurus beninensis Murray, 1855</li><li>Type locality: Old Calabar River, Nigeria.</li><li>Synonym: Malapterurus affinis</li><li>IUCN Red List: Least Concern (last assessed 2019).</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1259",
      "html": "<p>Africa: distributed along the coastal plain of west and central Africa from the lower Volta River (Ghana) to the Shiloango system (Angola and Congo DR), and on the Island of Fernando Po. Waters include the Cross, Cameroon's Sanaga and Lokunje.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1259",
      "html": "<p>Unreported in the aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1259",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: Malapterurus: From the Greek malakos, meaning soft, pteron, meaning fin and oura, meaning tail; presumably in reference to the adipose fin, which appears all the more prominent in the absence of the rayed dorsal fin.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
